    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Difference of squares",
        "body": "Let the populations be $a^2$, $a^2+100=b^2+1$ and $a^2+200=c^2$. From $b^2-a^2=99$: $(b-a)(b+a)=99=1\\cdot99=3\\cdot33=9\\cdot11$. Since $b-a$ and $b+a$ have the same parity, both are odd; the possible pairs give $a=49$ (from $1,99$), $a=15$ ($3,33$), $a=1$ ($9,11$). Now $a^2+200=c^2$: $49^2+200=2601=51^2$ ✓, while $15^2+200=425$ and $1+200=201$ are not squares. So $a=49$ and the original population is $49^2=2401=7^4$, a multiple of 7 (and of none of the other choices).\n\n**Answer: (B) 7**"
      }
